# pollux_nu: independent QA of `final_solution` (all checks pass) + threshold-shift analysis
## Format checks (`workspaces/pollux_nu/verify_final.py`)
`final_solution/submission.csv` (current candidate, global OOF **0.77778** [[alderamin_mu_status_07778.md]]):
- 463,243 rows, columns `contact_id,contact`; **unique**, **exact same set and order** as `data/sample_submission.csv`.
- binary `{0,1}`, 8,172 positives (1.764%); G rate 5.42%, player rate 1.42%.
- Recomputed OOF from `blend_{player,ground}_oof.npy` + metas = **0.777775**, exactly matching `blend_global_meta.json`. IDs/format are safe.
## Sanity of test predictions (no label leakage — train/test plays disjoint)
`workspaces/pollux_nu/qa_submission.py`:
- **all** predicted player positives are candidate pairs with tracking `dist<6` (median 0.78 yd; matches train positive median 0.83). Step distributions match train (player median step 28 vs train 27; G 54 vs train 53.5).
- predicted test rates are ~15% higher than OOF predicted rates (G 5.42% vs OOF predicted 4.67%; player 1.42% vs 1.24%). **Analysis:** per-play mean predicted probability correlates with per-play true positive rate (**Spearman .95 player / .82 ground**), and test per-play mean-prob distribution sits inside the OOF distribution (G test play median .052 vs OOF .028, OOF p95 .133). Conclusion: test plays are simply **more contact-heavy**, not miscalibrated → **keep the global OOF-optimal thresholds** (rate-matching thresholds would be wrong).
- Two/three test plays have 0 predicted G positives; train has plays with true G rate 0 and OOF play-rate p05=0, so this is expected for short/pass plays.
## Recommendation
Do **not** change thresholds or postprocess (morphology gains ≤0.0005, per-play/rank normalization negative — confirmed). Final candidate at **0.77778 OOF** looks robust; only integrate a new source if it adds >0.001 in the harness. I'll re-run the same QA on the very last `submission.csv`.
